INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - EARLY MORNING

A low-beamed room that smells of woodsmoke and old bread. Frost climbs the window glass. The range ticks as it warms. A pot of porridge sits uncovered at the back of the stove, a skin forming.

At the table, ANNELIESE FOSSEGARD-MBEKI, 17, in a school coat buttoned to the chin, sits upright with her satchel on her knees. Her boots are already laced. A rucksack waits by the door.

Her father, ANDREAS FOSSEGARD, 50s, thick-fingered, in his barn jacket, stands at the stove and stirs the porridge with a wooden spoon, round and round, long past the point of need.

ANDREAS
There's honey.

ANNELIESE
I'm fine.

ANDREAS
You'll want something in you for the bus.

ANNELIESE
I had toast.

ANDREAS
Toast isn't food.

ANNELIESE
It's what Mum used to say.

The spoon stops. Starts again. He lifts the pot and tips porridge into a bowl, sets it before her, and sits across, facing the door instead of her.

ANDREAS
Eat, Annie.

She lifts the spoon. Lowers it. A clock above the dresser ticks, loud as a hammer.

ANNELIESE
The coach leaves at seven.

ANDREAS
I know when it leaves.

ANNELIESE
It's twenty past six.

ANDREAS
I can tell time.

ANNELIESE
(muttering, to the porridge)
Could have fooled me.

He doesn't ask her to repeat it. His jaw works. Outside, a cow lows, and a gate bangs in the wind.

ANDREAS
Calves'll want feeding.

ANNELIESE
Jonas will do it.

ANDREAS
Jonas is eleven.

ANNELIESE
He's good with them.

ANDREAS
He's eleven, Annie.

ANNELIESE
I was eleven.

ANDREAS
And look what it got you.

She stands. The chair scrapes. She slings the satchel over her shoulder, crosses to the door, and picks up the rucksack. Her hand on the latch.

ANNELIESE
(to the empty hall, quietly)
Be ready, Annie.

ANDREAS
What did you say?

ANNELIESE
Nothing. I said I'll write.

ANDREAS
You won't.

ANNELIESE
I will.

ANDREAS
Your mother said she'd write.

The word lands. Anneliese stands very still with her back to him. The range ticks. The porridge sits, untouched.

ANNELIESE
She did write, Dad.

ANDREAS
Three letters in nine years.

ANNELIESE
You burned two.

ANDREAS
I did not.

ANNELIESE
I found the ash.

He is on his feet. The spoon clatters to the floor. Neither of them moves to pick it up.

ANDREAS
You've got your mother's mouth.

ANNELIESE
You've got her coat.

His eyes drop. He is wearing, under the barn jacket, a grey cardigan with a darned elbow. Her cardigan.

ANDREAS
Get out.

ANNELIESE
I'm going.

ANDREAS
Then go.

ANNELIESE
(opening the door)
Dad.

ANDREAS
Go, Annie. Go on.

She stands in the doorway, the white morning on her face. Doesn't turn.

ANNELIESE
Eat the porridge.

She steps out. The door swings shut. He stands in the silence, then lowers himself into her chair, takes up her spoon, and eats.
